  <info>
    <link type="guide" xref="index"/>
    <desc>What coyote does and how its main window is organized.</desc>
  </info>
  <title>Overview</title>
  <p>Coyote is a native LLM coding agent. It maintains a persistent conversation with a selected model, displays responses and tool activity, and can execute coding tasks through its configured tools.</p>
  <p>The main window contains the agents panel, conversation area, prompt area, Send and Stop controls, and a status area. Use the agents panel to organize the main agent and its short-lived subagents as a tree of virtual windows. Select an agent to display its conversation and, while it is live, make it the target of prompts and agent controls. Use the <link xref="send-prompt">Send a Prompt</link> task to begin a conversation.</p>
  <note><p>The agents panel is implemented in the development build. Coordinator-launched subagents remain short-lived: after their final response, they exit. Focused RPC qualification passes; display-backed and real-provider end-to-end qualification remain open. Completed agents remain available for review in the panel.</p></note>
  <links type="section"/>
  <p>Coordinator-launched subagents are shown as child virtual windows in the agents tree. Their processes remain short-lived and exit after the final response; the tree retains their conversation for review.</p>
  <section id="areas">
    <title>Main window areas</title>
    <p>For an explanation of a particular area, see <link xref="ui-conversation">Conversation area</link>, <link xref="ui-prompt">Prompt area</link>, or <link xref="ui-controls">Control area</link>.</p>
  </section>
